@davidfowl back up to the original scenario. Load balancer connection fixation has been a consistently reported problem in services and we've done small fixes to address it (e.g. #14522), but nothing consistent across servers.

For HTTP/1.1 an app usually mitigates this by adding `Connection: close` on random percentage of responses.
For HTTP/2 and 3 they they need to be able to trigger a GoAway. #14522 enabled this in HttpSys by converting a `Connection: close` to a GoAway.

@davidni have your issues been with HTTP/1.1, HTTP/2, or both?

We had a discussion about this today and as it turns out, we've already expose an API for this in Kestrel that work across HTTP/1, HTTP/2 and HTTP/3. We'll be expanding it to work on HTTP.sys and IIS as well. The [`IConnectionLifetimeNotificationFeature`](https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/microsoft.aspnetcore.connections.features.iconnectionlifetimenotificationfeature?view=aspnetcore-5.0) which has 0 documentation 😢  can be used to do this today.

Here's a sample:

```C#
public void Configure(IApplicationBuilder app, IWebHostEnvironment env)
{
    if (env.IsDevelopment())
    {
        app.UseDeveloperExceptionPage();
    }

    app.UseRouting();

    app.UseEndpoints(endpoints =>
    {
        endpoints.MapGet("/", async context =>
        {
            // This is the feature that allows you to trigger a graceful shutdown
            context.Features.Get<IConnectionLifetimeNotificationFeature>().RequestClose();
            await context.Response.WriteAsync("Hello World!");
        });
    });
}
```

It will send connection close on HTTP/1.1 connections, and it'll send a GOAWAY frame on HTTP/2. As part of this issue we're going to do a couple of things:

1. Expose `IConnectionLifetimeNotificationFeature` on HTTP.sys
2. Expose `IConnectionLifetimeNotificationFeature` on IIS
3. Expose a new API on `ConnectionInfo` to allow closing the requesting graceful connection closure.

```diff
namespace Microsoft.AspNetCore.Http
{
    public abstract class ConnectionInfo
    {
+      public virtual void RequestClose();
    }
}
```


```C#
public void Configure(IApplicationBuilder app, IWebHostEnvironment env)
{
    if (env.IsDevelopment())
    {
        app.UseDeveloperExceptionPage();
    }

    app.UseRouting();

    app.UseEndpoints(endpoints =>
    {
        endpoints.MapGet("/", async context =>
        {
            context.Connection.RequestClose();
            await context.Response.WriteAsync("Hello World!");
        });
    });
}
```

PS: As a workaround in the meantime, here's a polyfill for HTTP.sys:

```C#
public class ConnectionCloseNotificationLifeTimeFeature : IConnectionLifetimeNotificationFeature
{
    private readonly HttpResponse _httpResponse;

    public ConnectionCloseNotificationLifeTimeFeature(HttpResponse httpResponse)
    {
        _httpResponse = httpResponse;
    }

    public CancellationToken ConnectionClosedRequested { get; set; }

    public void RequestClose()
    {
        // Set the connection close feature if the response hasn't sent headers as yet
        if (!_httpResponse.HasStarted)
        {
            _httpResponse.Headers[HeaderNames.Connection] = "close";
        }
    }
}
```

Usage:

```C#
app.Use(next =>
{
    return context =>
    {
        var feature = context.Features.Get<IConnectionLifetimeNotificationFeature>();
        if (feature == null)
        {
            feature = new ConnectionCloseNotificationLifeTimeFeature(context.Response);
            context.Features.Set(feature);
        }

        return next(context);
    };
});
```
